// A test that FAILS on purpose, so an `Iter<T>` operand has its whole failure
// REPORT compared byte for byte.
//
// # Why it has to fail
//
// A PASSING assertion renders no operand at all, so there is no other way to
// observe an operand rendering. `tests/` contains no program that fails
// an assertion, so the differential sweep compares the passing path exclusively
// and would read `0 DIFFED` over any amount of wrong failure text. This file is
// NOT in the corpus and is never run by `nomi test tests`; it is reached
// only by TestPinned_IterOperandReport, and the non-zero exit is part of what
// requireIdentical compares. Same standing, and the same reason, as
// untyped_list_report.nomi.
//
// # Every operand is a NAME, deliberately
//
// The report suppresses an operand whose rendering reads exactly like its source
// text. An `Iter` expression written inline would therefore produce no row at
// all and this fixture would compare nothing. A BINDING forces the row, because
// `x` and `<iter>` do not read alike.
//
// # WHAT MAKES THE INFINITE CASE THE IMPORTANT ONE
//
// rt.IterInspectText is a CONSTANT rather than a rendering of the elements, and the
// second assertion is what makes that a testable property instead of a comment:
// `endless` is `Iter.from(0)`, an unbounded source. An inspector that rendered
// its elements would not produce wrong text — IT WOULD NOT RETURN. So this line
// distinguishes "the report shows a constant" from "the report shows the
// elements" in a way no finite source can, because over a finite source both
// designs terminate and only the string differs.
//
// The third assertion nests the sequence inside a List, which is the composite
// path: firstUninspectable reports the INNERMOST component with no inspector, so
// before rt.InspectSeq existed a `List<Iter<Int>>` was refused for the Iter and
// not for the list. With it, the list inspector composes over the element.
test "an Iter operand renders" {
    x = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5] |> Iter.filter(|n| n > 2)

    assert Iter.to_list(x) == [3, 4, 5, 9]
}

test "an unbounded Iter operand renders without being consumed" {
    endless = Iter.from(0)

    assert Iter.count(Iter.take(endless, 3)) == 99
}

test "an Iter nested in a List renders" {
    x = [1, 2] |> Iter.map(|n| n * 2)

    pair = [x, x]

    assert Iter.count(pair) == 99
}
